# AI Execution Framework Rules

These rules apply to ALL agents working in this project. They are non-negotiable invariants.

## Source of Truth

- `ai-framework/LOGIC.md` is the authoritative workflow specification. Follow it exactly.
- `ai-framework/project-profile.md` defines language-specific conventions. Read it before any work.
- `ARCHITECTURE.md` at the project root defines the domain dependency policy. All agents must respect the domain dependency DAG. Circular cross-domain dependencies are strictly prohibited.
- Taskwarrior state is the source of truth for execution progress, not filesystem layout or git status.
- All Taskwarrior read-only commands must use `taskwarrior/tw`, never bare `task`. This ensures per-project database isolation.

## Hierarchy

The framework uses a three-level hierarchy:

- **Milestone** (optional): groups related epics for release planning
- **Epic**: the unit of parallel execution; gets its own git worktree; contains ordered stories
- **Story**: a vertical feature slice executed serially within its epic

Epics run in parallel (separate worktrees). Stories within an epic run serially (same worktree).

## Script-Only State Mutations

Agents must NEVER call `taskwarrior/tw` directly for state changes (modify, add, done, start, stop, annotate, denotate, delete). All state mutations go through the provided scripts under `taskwarrior/`:

- PM uses: `pm-lock-acquire`, `pm-lock-release`, `epic-fork`, `epic-merge`, `epic-mark-ready`, `epic-rebase`, `epic-gate-release`
- Coordinator uses: `coordinator-lock-acquire`, `coordinator-lock-release`, `story-init`, `story-next`, `story-complete`, `story-merge`, `phase-start`, `phase-stop`, `phase-transition`, `phase-annotate`, `phase-done`, `phase-block`
- Phase agents use: `phase-annotate`, `phase-transition`
- Read-only queries (epic-status, pm-preflight, epic-gate-status, coordinator-lock-status, `taskwarrior/tw ... export/count`) are allowed directly.

Scripts enforce preconditions, validate transitions, and exit non-zero on violations. Agents react to exit codes.

## State Discipline

- Agents must never advance more than one state transition per invocation (e.g. plan -> plan-review, not plan -> write).
- Every artifact written must be annotated on the corresponding Taskwarrior task via `phase-annotate`.
- The Coordinator is the only agent that creates Taskwarrior tasks (via `story-init`), marks tasks done (via `phase-done`), or manages git branches.
- At most one Taskwarrior task may be `+ACTIVE -AI_LOCK` at any time per worktree. The Coordinator verifies this via `phase-start` (which exits 1 if another task is active).
- Only one PM may run at a time (global lock in main tree). Use `pm-lock-acquire`.
- Only one Coordinator may run per epic worktree. Use `coordinator-lock-acquire`.
- Duplicate PM/Coordinator agents must not modify state. They report status and exit.
- Stale locks and orphaned active tasks must only be cleared by the user after confirming no agents are running, via `ccmd bash taskwarrior/cleanup-ai-state.sh --apply`.

## Merge Gate

- Only one epic may merge to main at a time. The merge gate is enforced by `epic-merge`.
- No new epic worktrees may be created while a merge is in progress (`epic-fork` checks the gate).
- The PM never directly manipulates the gate. Only `epic-merge` and `epic-fork` scripts interact with it.
- Stale gate recovery: `ccmd bash taskwarrior/epic-gate-release --force` (manual only, after confirming no merge is in progress).

## Review Standards

- Review agents must cite specific files and issues. "Looks good" without evidence is forbidden.
- Review agents must not nitpick formatting or style. Focus on correctness, completeness, and structural integrity.
- The Coordinator enforces the 3-rejection limit per phase and writes an escalation on the 3rd rejection.

## Implementation Standards

- Implementation agents must never: hardcode test values, write empty stubs, use mocks in production code, skip error handling, or silently deviate from the architecture.
- If implementation is impossible given the architecture, escalate. Do not silently work around it.

## Context Discipline

- The PM never reads source code, tests, or plan artifacts below the story level, except `plan/discoveries/` during Discovery Triage.
- The Coordinator never reads code, requirements, architecture, tests, or review content. It only reads Taskwarrior output from scripts.
- Phase agents read only what their role specifies.

## Escalation

- If an agent cannot complete its task, it must write an escalation file to `plan/escalations/`, annotate the task via `ccmd bash taskwarrior/phase-annotate`, and **exit immediately**.
- Escalation files use the template from `plan/templates/escalation.md`.
- Escalations halt Coordinator work. The Coordinator blocks the task via `phase-block`, releases its lock, and returns control to the PM.
- The PM may invoke `escalation-recovery` only after verifying the Coordinator lock is free in the worktree.

## Discoveries

- Any agent may write one new discovery file under `plan/discoveries/` using `plan/templates/discovery.md`, then continue its task.
- Discovery filenames: `YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS-slug.md`.
- Subagents must NEVER read, list, search, deduplicate, modify, or delete existing discovery files.
- Only the PM triages discoveries after milestone completion.

## Git

- One worktree per epic, branched from main (`epic/EXXXX-slug`).
- One branch per story within the worktree (`story/XXXXX-slug`).
- Commit after phase review approval only (except PM committing planning artifacts to main).
- Never force-push or rebase shared branches.

## Entry Point Enforcement

This framework only works when the `project-manager` agent drives the pipeline. If you are NOT the `project-manager`, `coordinator`, `fixer`, `escalation-recovery`, or a named phase agent, you are a **general agent**:

- You must NOT write code, requirements, architecture artifacts, tests, or framework artifacts.
- You must NOT invoke the Coordinator or phase agents yourself.
- If the user asks to implement a feature or "run the Coordinator," tell them to open a new chat with the **`project-manager`** agent.

Only designated phase-write agents may create or modify files in their respective directories.

The `escalation-recovery` agent may modify artifacts only for bounded corrections to the current story, and only when the PM invokes it after a clean Coordinator halt.

The `fixer` agent may modify source and test files for bug fixes only. It does not use Taskwarrior and operates outside the PM pipeline.

## Forbidden

- Read and respect the "Forbidden" section in `ai-framework/project-profile.md`.
